::ENG3_U06_L08_GP01::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker presents a research-based literary claim to classmates.
Which choice best supports oral communication?\{
~A. Begin with unrelated personal comments.#Incorrect. Unrelated comments weaken focus and do not support the claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. State a clear claim, use credible evidence, and organize the explanation logically.#Correct. Oral communication requires focus, credible evidence, and organization. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Use only images and no spoken explanation.#Incorrect. Oral communication still needs clear spoken explanation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Avoid explaining the evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence must be explained so the audience understands the reasoning. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A digital presentation includes a chart from research.
What makes the chart effective?\{
~A. It is colorful but unrelated.#Incorrect. Color does not help if the chart does not support the point. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. It replaces the speaker's explanation.#Incorrect. Multimedia supports explanation but does not replace it. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. It includes every detail from the research notes.#Incorrect. Too much detail can overwhelm the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. It is labeled and connected to the claim being presented.#Correct. Labels and clear connection improve audience understanding. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes a short written summary to a class LAN page.
Which final check is most important?\{
=A. The writing is revised, audience-aware, and supported by purposeful multimedia.#Correct. Publication readiness requires quality writing, audience fit, and integrated media. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The page includes as many images as possible.#Incorrect. More images do not guarantee clarity. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The writing is copied from the rough draft without review.#Incorrect. Publication requires revision. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The product ignores reader needs.#Incorrect. Audience awareness is central to publication. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP04::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: Feedback says a paragraph has a good claim but unclear organization.
What revision best addresses the issue?\{
~A. Change only the background color.#Incorrect. Color does not fix organization. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Add unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. Unrelated evidence can make organization worse. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Reorder the sentences so the claim, evidence, and explanation follow a clear path.#Correct. Revision should improve clarity and structure. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Remove the claim entirely.#Incorrect. The claim gives the paragraph focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP05::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A caption under a presentation image has punctuation and capitalization errors.
Why should it be corrected?\{
~A. Captions do not matter in digital work.#Incorrect. Captions are part of the communication product. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Errors can distract the audience and weaken credibility.#Correct. Standard English conventions support clarity and trust. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Errors make the caption more interesting.#Incorrect. Errors usually reduce clarity. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The image replaces the caption completely.#Incorrect. Captions often explain how media supports the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
